al_fusion_dictionary.tsv and al_fusion_anchors.tsv
==================================================

Two files, because acute leukaemia needs two kinds of prior and myeloma only
needed one.

1. al_fusion_dictionary.tsv - NAMED PAIRS
-----------------------------------------
One row per recurrent gene pair. Columns:

  partner_a, partner_b   Gene symbols. Order is irrelevant.
  name                   Cytogenetic notation, e.g. t(8;21)(q22;q22.1)
  disease                AML, ALL, or BOTH. Rows are filtered to the panel in
                         use, so an ALL run does not report AML entities.
  tier                   defining   WHO-5 / ICC disease-defining
                         risk       ELN 2022 risk-assigning
                         actionable drives a specific drug
                         emerging   described but not classification-defining
  frequency              Approximate, for context in the report
  entity                 WHO-5 / ICC entity name
  partner_b_band         Expected cytoband of partner_b. Only needed when
                         partner_b is off-panel. See "partial matching" below.
  references             WHO-5, ICC 2022, ELN 2022, or a PMID
  notes                  Free text

MATCHING. Partner names are not compared to BED labels as strings. Each name
is reduced to a set of gene tokens (split on / and _, uppercased, with
region-suffix words like "locus" dropped) and two partners match when their
token sets intersect. So IGH_locus matches a BED label of IGH, and TAL1/STIL
matches either TAL1 or STIL. Order within a pair and within a compound is
irrelevant. Implemented in bin/annotate_al_fusions.py (norm_tokens,
dictionary_lookup).

PARTIAL MATCHING. Two defining B-ALL entities have a partner that is off-panel
by design: PBX1 in TCF3::PBX1, and DUX4 in IGH::DUX4. When one side of a
junction is on-panel and the other resolves only to a cytoband, a row carrying
a partner_b_band equal to that band still names the event, recorded as
match_quality=partial_band. Band comparison is tiered - exact sub-band, then
major band, then arm - because T2T-CHM13 band boundaries differ from GRCh38's
and exact-string matching would lose a defining entity to a one-band shift.
An arm-level agreement is recorded as partial_arm and should be read as a
lead, not a call.

2. al_fusion_anchors.tsv - PROMISCUOUS ANCHORS
----------------------------------------------
A named-pair dictionary cannot work for KMT2A, which has over a hundred
described partners, or NUP98, which has more than thirty, or the Ig and TR
loci, whose partner is the whole point and is frequently novel. Missing a
KMT2A junction because its partner was not in a list would be a clinically
material false negative.

An anchor is reported whenever it appears on either side of a junction,
whatever the partner is, including when the partner is off-panel or resolves
only to a cytoband. Columns:

  anchor          Gene or locus label
  anchor_class    promiscuous_5prime, promiscuous_3prime, enhancer_hijack,
                  kinase_ABL_class, kinase_JAK_class, kinase_other,
                  tandem_duplication, enhancer_target
  disease         AML, ALL, or BOTH
  partner_policy  "any" for partner-agnostic reporting; "self" for
                  intragenic events (UBTF-TD, MYB duplication)
  notes           Free text

EDITING
-------
Adding a pair or an anchor needs no code change. Keep partner names as real
gene symbols: matching is token-based, so an invented joined token will not
resolve. The only special word the normaliser strips is "locus".

CURATION STATUS
---------------
Rows with references beginning "panel:" carry the panel design's own
designation rather than a citation, and have not been independently verified.
They are all tier=emerging. Curate them against your source before using them
for clinical reporting.

Rows whose partner is absent from the corresponding panel BED carry a note
saying so and a partner_b_band so they remain resolvable. Four of these mark
genes present on one panel but not the other despite being relevant to both -
LYN, NTRK3, ABL2 and EWSR1 - and are flagged in assets/PANEL_README.md as
candidate additions rather than silently worked around.

ORIENTATION RULE FOR partner_b_band
-----------------------------------
When partner_b_band is set, partner_a MUST be the side that is on the panel
and partner_b the side that is not. The single-sided lookup matches the
observed on-panel gene against partner_a only, then compares the other
breakpoint's cytoband against partner_b_band. A row with the off-panel gene in
partner_a can never match, silently.

check_panel_consistency.py --dictionary catches this: it reports every
partner_a not present on the panel, so a mis-oriented row surfaces as a
"named by the fusion dictionary but not on this panel" warning rather than
failing quietly at report time.
